# Gavel B made an err unhandleable — it rose to the endpoint and dispatching on
# it was a compile error — so a program that wanted to know whether some bytes
# were valid utf-8 had to test before converting. That is why the July err-arm
# migration plan carried a `valid_utf8` predicate as its one additive item.
#
# Gavel 1 (2026-08-15) replaced the absolute with the foreign-only rescue
# license, and `std/text` is foreign to every program that imports it, so the
# question can be asked of the conversion itself. The predicate has nothing left
# to do, and the plan that wanted it was deleted on 2026-08-25.
#
# tests/golden/runtime/utf8_of_a_slice_names_the_wrapper.kso pins the other
# side of this — the same refusal reaching the endpoint when nobody asks.
import "std/text"

fn told (err _)
  "refused"

fn told _
  "ok"

pub play =
  print "{told (text/utf8 [104 105])}"
  >> print "{told (text/utf8 [255])}"
